      "id": "ta4-17",
      "part": "ta",
      "passage": "<p>(1) For many teenagers, bedtime is a battle. Homework, messages and videos compete for attention, and sleep is often the first thing to be cut. Yet scientists increasingly warn that sleep is not a luxury but a basic need, especially during adolescence.</p><p>(2) During sleep, the brain sorts and stores what has been learned during the day. A student who sleeps well after studying is more likely to remember the material than one who stays up late. Sleep also affects mood: people who do not get enough sleep are more likely to feel irritable and anxious, and they find it harder to control their emotions.</p><p>(3) The problem is partly biological. At puberty, the body clock shifts later, so teenagers naturally feel sleepy later at night and want to wake later in the morning. School start times, however, often remain early. The result is a long-term sleep debt that many students carry through the week.</p><p>(4) Screens make the problem worse. Light from a phone or tablet can delay the release of melatonin, a hormone that signals the body to sleep. Many teenagers also use social media at night, and the fear of missing out keeps them online longer than they intended.</p><p>(5) Experts suggest practical steps: keep a regular bedtime, dim the lights an hour before sleep, and leave phones outside the bedroom. Schools can help by teaching sleep education and, where possible, starting lessons later. None of these changes is dramatic, but together they can make a real difference. Sleep, after all, is when the brain does some of its most important work.</p>",
